# The first check accepts raw int-reference data without executing it.
# The second wrapper requires a Structure reference and refuses the int s.
# Step 21.7 K2, S3: the record Types is written inside Bad and launched by `Bad\Types()` over its data
# parent(Types) = Bad. Its first line, `@: p int`, accepts the reference producer `@: p int data[touch()]` of Bad
# without reading it; the mark written after it shows that the first line passed (p_checked 1). The second line,
# `@: s A`, is the reference s that initially refers to the Structure A: in code the line checks the destination on
# its own line, the place s of the data already holds the int 99, and the line answers 4 before any effect --
# `Observed\after` is not written. `touch` records each of its runs in the graph (`Observed\touches`); it is run once
# before the launch as the control of that record (touches 1) and the launch adds none (still 1).
# The former record `call(Bad; Types\code)` still fits the outer form [args, code] with Bad as the args actual; the
# substitution of DATA is removed (the data is parent(Types)), and Bad's fields name no input of it: it answers 5.
# Norm: the book #structural-call (the data is parent(C)), the roles of data and code (#data-code-roles: «in code it
# checks the type on its own line»); the L2 spec #lowlevel-address (`@: s A` initially refers to A itself).
Observed:
    int: before 0
    int: after 0
    int: touches 0
    int: p_checked 0
end: Observed
fn: touch () int
    Observed\touches := Observed\touches + 1
    return: 0
A:
    int: v 7
end: A
Bad:
    @: p int data[touch()]
    int: s 99
    Types:
        @: p int
        Observed\p_checked := 1
        @: s A
    end: Types
end: Bad
Main:
    int: control touch()
    Observed\before := 1
    Bad\Types()
    Observed\after := 1
